5. THE THREE PRIMARY LOCKING MODES

The entire application must be organized around three distinct modes.

These modes have different timing behavior.

They must never be confused or implemented as variations of the same countdown.

MODE A — INSTANT LOCK

Purpose

Instant Lock immediately restricts the phone for a duration selected by the user.

Configuration

The user selects:

- Lock Duration.
- Allowed Applications.
- Phone Call Access.
- Recovery Code.

Example:

- Lock Duration: 1 hour.
- Allowed Apps: 3 applications.
- Phone Calls: Enabled.
- Recovery: 16-digit code.

Expected behavior

1. User selects Instant Lock.
2. User configures the required options.
3. User reviews the configuration.
4. User presses Start Instant Lock.
5. RESOLVE prepares the enforcement policy.
6. RESOLVE enters the supported Android Lock Task Mode.
7. The full-screen Resolve Lock Screen appears immediately.
8. The restricted-session timer starts after enforcement has been confirmed.
9. Normal Home Screen access is restricted.
10. Only permitted applications and necessary Phone functionality remain accessible.
11. The user can enter the recovery code.
12. After the configured lock duration expires, RESOLVE exits the restriction state.

Example timeline

User selects a one-hour Instant Lock.

At the moment the session successfully enters Lock Task Mode:

"LOCK START = 00:00"

The phone remains restricted for one hour.

When the hour ends:

"LOCK COMPLETE"

Normal device usability is restored through supported Android mechanisms.

Important rules

- No preliminary free-use period.
- No unnecessary 10-second, 30-second, or 60-second startup countdown.
- No duplicate lock triggers.
- No fake active-security indicator.
- No timer reset caused by screen recreation.

---

MODE B — DELAYED LOCK

This is a core differentiating feature of RESOLVE.

Delayed Lock is designed to prevent users from continuing to use distracting applications longer than the time they originally intended.

It allows the phone to remain normally usable for a chosen period, then automatically enters restricted Lock Mode.

Delayed Lock is not a short countdown before locking.

It contains two independent durations.

Duration 1: FREE USE WINDOW

The period during which the phone remains normally usable.

Duration 2: LOCK DURATION

The period during which the phone remains restricted after the Free Use Window ends.

Both must be configurable independently.

Example configuration

The user selects:

- Mode: Delayed Lock.
- Free Use Window: 30 minutes.
- Lock Duration: 1 hour.
- Allowed Apps During Lock: 5 applications.
- Phone Calls: Enabled.
- Recovery Code: 16 digits.

Exact expected behavior

T = 00:00 — Delayed Lock begins

The user confirms the configuration.

RESOLVE stores the session configuration and schedules the automatic lock transition.

The phone remains normally usable.

The user may use:

- Facebook.
- YouTube.
- Games.
- Browsers.
- Messaging applications.
- Other installed applications.

The selected Allowed Apps list does not restrict normal usage during this period.

The Free Use Window is not itself a restricted session.

T = 00:30 — Free Use Window expires

The transition deadline arrives.

RESOLVE must automatically initiate the actual Lock Mode transition.

The user must not need to:

- Open RESOLVE manually.
- Press another button.
- Confirm a popup.
- Return to the Home Screen.
- Activate Instant Lock manually.

Automatic transition

RESOLVE must use a documented and supported Android mechanism to bring its Lock Activity into the foreground and establish actual Lock Task Mode.

The previously active non-allowlisted application must become inaccessible.

The user should see the full-screen RESOLVE Lock Screen.

After transition — LOCK DURATION begins

The one-hour restricted-session countdown starts only after the enforcement state has been successfully established.

During this period:

- Ordinary unapproved third-party applications are restricted.
- The normal Home Screen is inaccessible through ordinary navigation.
- Selected applications remain usable.
- Phone calls remain accessible.
- The configured recovery code can terminate the session.
- The countdown continues accurately.

T = 01:30 — Lock Duration expires

RESOLVE exits the supported enforcement state.

Normal phone usage is restored.

Important timing rules

The two timers are independent.

The Free Use Window begins when Delayed Lock is confirmed.

The Lock Duration begins only when Lock Task enforcement becomes active.

Do not start both countdowns simultaneously.

Do not subtract the Free Use Window from the Lock Duration.

Do not automatically restart a new Free Use Window after the phone is reopened.

Do not transform Delayed Lock into per-app usage tracking.

The entire device becomes restricted when the Free Use Window ends.

Additional delayed-lock requirements

Allow custom Free Use durations such as:

- 10 minutes.
- 15 minutes.
- 30 minutes.
- 45 minutes.
- 1 hour.
- 2 hours.
- Custom durations.

The user must be able to configure any valid custom duration within the application's documented limits.

