6. REUSABLE AND RELIABLE TIME CONFIGURATION SYSTEM

All three locking modes must use a single consistent duration-selection design.

Do not create three different timer interfaces.

Primary design objective

Time configuration should be easy, direct, visually engaging, and quick.

The user should not have to repeatedly press plus/minus buttons to configure durations.

6.1 Main Duration Display

Use a large, elegant duration display.

Example:

"03 : 40"

Hours and minutes must be clearly distinguishable.

The user should be able to understand the selected duration immediately.

6.2 Independent Drag-Based Selection

Create a smooth interactive wheel-based time selector or an equally usable custom drag selector.

Provide separate controls for:

- Hours.
- Minutes.

The controls must operate independently.

Users should be able to drag either value without unexpectedly changing the other.

CRITICAL BUG TO FIX

The previous time picker had a state-management bug.

Example:

The user wants to select:

"3 hours 40 minutes"

But the existing implementation behaves incorrectly:

- Selecting 1 hour unexpectedly changes minutes to 59.
- Selecting minutes resets or changes the hour value.
- The selected duration shifts during interaction.
- The hour and minute values do not remain independently selected.

This is unacceptable.

Required solution

Use one authoritative duration representation, preferably total minutes stored as a Long integer, with carefully controlled derived hour and minute values.

Alternatively, maintain separate authoritative hour and minute fields with a single validated reducer.

In either design:

- Changing hours must preserve minutes.
- Changing minutes must preserve hours.
- Both controls must update the same session configuration.
- The displayed total duration must always match the selected values.
- UI recomposition must never reset either value.
- Dragging one wheel must not recreate or reset the other wheel.
- State restoration must preserve the exact duration.

Example:

Initial state:

"00:00"

Select 3 hours:

"03:00"

Then select 40 minutes:

"03:40"

The final duration must remain exactly 3 hours and 40 minutes.

The reverse selection order must also work.

Do not introduce hidden rounding or automatic changes to 59 minutes.

Allow minute selection in one-minute increments so the user can select values such as 17, 25, 40, or 57 minutes.

6.3 Direct Numeric Entry

Allow the user to tap the large duration display.

Open a clean numeric-entry interface.

Allow direct entry of:

- Hours.
- Minutes.

Example:

"3h 40m"

Validate the values.

After confirmation, update the drag selector and the session configuration.

Both interfaces must remain synchronized.

6.4 Quick Presets

Provide compact presets such as:

- 10m.
- 15m.
- 30m.
- 45m.
- 1h.
- 2h.
- 3h.
- 4h.

Presets are optional shortcuts, not the primary interface.

Custom durations must remain available.

6.5 Reuse Across All Modes

Instant Lock:

- Configure Lock Duration.

Delayed Lock:

- Configure Free Use Window.
- Configure Lock Duration.

Schedule Lock:

- Configure Lock Duration.
- Configure scheduled start date/time.

Use the same reusable duration component.

Do not create separate timer-selection logic for each mode.

